\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 STAR aligner
\f1\b0\fs24 \
very fast aligner with large RAM consumption --> 30-40 GB! for human genomes!!\
	possible with lower RAM requirements with proper reference index \
aligns across splice junctions and discovers new putative splice sites\
\
\pard\tx566\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 approach
\f1\b0 :\
- reads are split into pieces (default 50bp)\
- read splits are aligned perfectly \
- these seeds are stitched together\
\

\f0\b create ref genome:
\f1\b0 \
!! it is highly recommended to have a properly annotated gtf-file (is there for human) that stores the transcript and feature info!!\
\
STAR --runMode genomeGenerate\
	--genomeDir				path to genomeFasta files\
	--sjdbGTFfile 				path to .gtf annotation file\
	--sjdbOverhang	99			should be your readlength - 1\
[	--sjdbFileChrStart			path to star-formatted annotation file gtf.sjfb) ] \
	--runThreadN n				number of threads to use\
\

\f0\b run alignment
\f1\b0 \
STAR \'97runMode \
	\'97readFilesIn sampleA-r1,sampleB-r1,sampleC-r1 sampleA-r2,sampleB-r2,sampleC-r2\
	# samples are separated by commas - reads are separated by space \
	\'97genomeDir <path>			path to star-index folder\
	\'97readFilesCommand <cmd>	use \'84zcat\'94 for gzipped fastq.gz files or bunzip2 -c for .bz files\
	\'97runThreadN <N>			multicore processing\
	\'97outSAMtype \
		BAM SortedByCoordinate		output sorted bam\
		SAM Unsorted				output unsorted sam\
	\'97outFileNamePrefix				path to output folder}